WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.051 --> 00:00:00.920
(upbeat music)

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:02.950
- [Sawako] We're not
trying to make everything

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:04.995
half Jewish and half Japanese.

00:00:04.995 --> 00:00:05.828
(bell dings)

00:00:05.828 --> 00:00:08.303
It'd be easy to call it Jewish-Japanese,

00:00:08.303 --> 00:00:09.940
but it's more than that.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.030
- [Aaron] It comes from
a very genuine place.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.350
I think our food is a lot
about building bridges,

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.261
not about barriers between cultures.

00:00:16.261 --> 00:00:18.844
(upbeat music)

00:00:21.734 --> 00:00:24.040
(bright music)

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:25.900
- [Sawako] My name is Sawako Okochi.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.470
I'm a chef owner of Shalom Japan.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.140
- My name is Aaron Isarael,

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.217
and I'm also a chef and
owner of Shalom Japan.

00:00:33.217 --> 00:00:35.053
- So, we've been together for

00:00:35.053 --> 00:00:36.428
eight years.
- Eight years.

00:00:36.428 --> 00:00:39.160
- [Aaron] Shalom Japan is our restaurant.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.538
The food is a collaboration
between the two of us.

00:00:42.538 --> 00:00:44.930
- [Sawako] I grew up in Hiroshima,

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:47.630
and I moved to New York
to go to culinary school.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:50.120
- [Aaron] I grew up in Great
Neck, New York, which is...

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.626
- Jewish.
(both laughing)

00:00:51.626 --> 00:00:53.240
- So Jewish.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:57.240
We grew the restaurant
organically out of being dating,

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:01.183
and then, living together
and sharing different foods.

00:01:01.183 --> 00:01:03.340
I really wanted to open my own restaurant,

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:06.867
and so, I think we just decided
at one point, we're like,

00:01:06.867 --> 00:01:08.135
"Hey, let's just do it together."

00:01:08.135 --> 00:01:10.718
(bright music)

00:01:12.123 --> 00:01:15.456
(metal door clattering)

00:01:16.772 --> 00:01:18.760
(quirky music)

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.580
- [Sawako] Shalom Japan has
been open for six years.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:24.680
One of the first dishes
that we put on the menu

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:25.810
is a lox bowl.

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:28.360
- [Aaron] Our lox bowl is
our version of chirashi,

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.393
which is a Japanese dish of
raw fish over sushi rice.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:36.610
We put our house-cured
lox over sushi rice,

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.460
and then we put a whole bunch of

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.320
traditional Japanese ingredients,

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.600
and then also, some Jewish ingredients.

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:46.888
So, Japanese pickles, fried
capers, avocado and cilantro.

00:01:46.888 --> 00:01:50.010
Our matzo ball ramen is our version

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:51.930
of a great, classic Jewish soup.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.970
And then, has the ramen
noodles, matzo ball,

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:59.250
has a little gyoza that we fill
with chicken and foie gras,

00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:00.600
and a little garlic-chili oil.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.010
Grandma didn't put garlic chili oil

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.530
in the matzo ball soup in my house.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:08.351
Sake kasu challah is a, challah's
a traditonal Jewish bread,

00:02:08.351 --> 00:02:09.860
almost like a brioche,

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.420
and we bake sake kasu into it.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.530
Sake kasu is the leftover
mash when you make sake,

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:18.046
so kind of yeasty, kind of fermented,

00:02:18.046 --> 00:02:19.653
kind of a little funky.

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.770
(quirky music)

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:28.200
Our food seems like an
unlikely combination.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.090
It's sort of who we are.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:30.090
It's what we cook at home,

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:32.050
with the experiences we've had together,

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:33.877
how we kind of, take those and then

00:02:33.877 --> 00:02:36.500
express them at our restaurant.

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:38.623
- I hear that people
are pleasantly surprised

00:02:38.623 --> 00:02:41.607
because people come in with
like, "What is this place?

00:02:41.607 --> 00:02:43.240
"A funny name, Shalom Japan."

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:46.190
And they have changed their
mind by the end of the meal.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:47.710
You know, that was delicious,

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.320
never had anything like this, you know?

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.370
Like, that's the response we get a lot,

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:53.220
and that's what gets us going.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:54.570
- I think our food is not about

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:56.140
putting up barriers between cultures

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.410
but looking at how can we
relate to each other, like,

00:02:58.410 --> 00:02:59.738
through those ingredients.

00:02:59.738 --> 00:03:02.321
(bright music)

00:03:04.715 --> 00:03:07.215
(bell echoes)

